window.CPC_BUSCAR_EN = {"201":{"title":"Find it in the book 1","type":"buscar","questions":[{"q":"«Explain FIVE criteria the company will have to meet to obtain the correct operator's licence.»","ans":[[10,"Transport Operator Licensing Requirements / Criteria (good repute, financial standing, competence, establishment)"]],"exp":"Good repute, financial standing, professional competence (CPC) and a stable and effective establishment; they must be met at all times. Write one sentence with a verb for each, applied to the case."},
{"q":"«The operator wants to add 3 coaches to its licence. How much additional financial standing must it demonstrate?»","ans":[[10,"Transport Operator Licensing Requirements / Criteria (good repute, financial standing, competence, establishment)"]],"exp":"€9,000 for the first vehicle and €5,000 for each additional one: 3 × €5,000 = €15,000 more. The detail is on p. 8."},
{"q":"«The company's transport manager has resigned. Explain what the company must do and any time limits.»","ans":[[10,"Two types of transport manager"]],"exp":"Tell the Department of Transport the date they left the post and appoint another one as soon as possible: the Department can allow up to 6 months. Otherwise, the licence can be suspended or revoked."},
{"q":"«A contracted transport manager already manages four undertakings. Can he act for your company as well?»","ans":[[10,"Two types of transport manager"]],"exp":"No: a contracted manager can look after a maximum of 4 companies and 50 vehicles in total (p. 10)."},
{"q":"«A school uses its own bus, driven by its own staff, to take pupils on trips. Does it need a Road Passenger Transport Operator Licence?»","ans":[[10,"…types exempt from the licence (paragraph)"]],"exp":"No: schools with their own vehicles, companies carrying their own workers, not-for-profit voluntary organisations and anyone who does not charge for carrying passengers are exempt."},
{"q":"«The company will start coach tours to France. Which licence documents must it hold, and which must be carried on each coach?»","ans":[[10,"…International / Community Licence (paragraph)"]],"exp":"International licence and Community Licence, kept at the base; in each coach, the certified copy of the licence with the disc on the windscreen and the certified copy of the Community Licence. The manager needs the international CPC."},
{"q":"«To run a scheduled bus route, which three licences are needed: for the operator, the vehicle and the route? Who issues each one?»","ans":[[10,"Overview of Passenger Transport Operator Licensing"]],"exp":"Operator: operator licence (RTOL, Department of Transport). Vehicle: LPSV licence (Garda PSV Officer). Route: Public Bus Passenger Service Licence (NTA)."},
{"q":"«Draw up a legal schedule. At what point must the driver take the 45-minute break?»","ans":[[11,"max driving before a break · breaks"]],"exp":"After 4 h 30 of driving: 45 consecutive minutes, or 15 and then 30. Also remember the working time break after 6 hours of work."},
{"q":"«The driver has already driven 10 hours on two days this week. Can he drive 10 hours again on Friday?»","ans":[[11,"daily · weekly · fortnightly driving"]],"exp":"No: 9 hours a day, extendable to 10 only twice a week. Also: 56 hours a week and 90 in two weeks."},
{"q":"«Two drivers share the coach on an overnight journey to Sorrento. What daily rest must each of them take?»","ans":[[11,"daily rest · double manned"]],"exp":"Each driver gets 9 hours of rest within each 30-hour period, with the vehicle stationary. The co-driver can count the first 45 minutes as a break; the rest is availability."},
{"q":"«The driver's weekly rest was reduced to 24 hours. When must the difference be made up?»","ans":[[11,"weekly rest"]],"exp":"Regular: 45 h; reduced: 24 h. The difference is made up en bloc before the end of the third following week."},
{"q":"«The driver unloads luggage for 2 hours after a 3-hour drive, then drives another hour. When is a working time break required?»","ans":[[11,"breaks"],[11,"Working Time for EU-Regulated Drivers"]],"exp":"After 6 hours of work (driving + other work) a break is due: 30 minutes if the day is 6 to 9 h and 45 if it is over 9. It is the book's example, p. 5 and p. 7."},
{"q":"«One of the drivers works between 01.00 and 05.00. What is the maximum working time in that 24-hour period?»","ans":[[11,"night work · records"]],"exp":"Any work between 01:00 and 05:00 makes the driver a night worker: a maximum of 10 hours of work in those 24 hours, unless there is a collective agreement. Records: 2 years."},
{"q":"«The urban bus route is 25 km long. Which drivers' hours rules apply to it?»","ans":[[11,"Domestic Regulations"]],"exp":"Regular services up to 50 km are outside 561/2006: domestic rules apply. 5 h 30 of driving, 10 h a day, a 16 h working day and 10 h of rest."},
{"q":"«A driver drives a local route in the morning and a coach excursion in the afternoon. How do the two sets of rules combine?»","ans":[[11,"Mixed EU/Domestic Driving"]],"exp":"Time driven under EU rules counts towards the domestic limits; domestic driving never counts as an EU break or rest (it is recorded as other work). That day: a maximum of 10 h of driving and a 16 h working day."},
{"q":"«Explain how the 12-day derogation could be used on a coach tour to Italy, and its conditions.»","ans":[[11,"weekly rest"]],"exp":"A single international occasional service with at least 24 h in another country: the weekly rest can be postponed until day 12. Before, 45 h; after, 90 h (or 45 + 24). Digital tachograph; at night, two drivers or a break every 3 h. It continues on p. 4."},
{"q":"«Showing all your workings, calculate the total price to be charged for the job, including the profit requirement.»","ans":[[14,"Vehicle Utilisation"],[14,"Terminology"]],"exp":"Price = days × fixed cost per day + km × cost per km + expenses, then the profit %. The cost per day is the fixed costs divided by 225 days. The rate calculations start on p. 11-12."},
{"q":"«Calculate the annual straight-line depreciation of the new coach.»","ans":[[14,"Straight Line Depreciation"]],"exp":"(Price − tyres − residual value) ÷ years of life. The tyres are subtracted because they are a variable cost."},
{"q":"«Depreciate the coach at 20% per year on the reducing balance method.»","ans":[[14,"Reducing Balance Depreciation"]],"exp":"Each year: what is left × 20%. Start from the price without tyres and do not subtract the residual value."},
{"q":"«Allocate €96,000 of administration costs between the 12 coaches in the fleet.»","ans":[[14,"Apportioning Administration Costs – Payload Basis"]],"exp":"Per vehicle: 96,000 ÷ 12 = €8,000 each. The book also explains allocation by tonnes and by km; for similar buses, by vehicle."},
{"q":"«Calculate the fuel cost and the tyre cost per kilometre.»","ans":[[14,"Vehicle Running Costs"]],"exp":"Fuel = €/litre ÷ km per litre. Tyres = (no. of tyres × price) ÷ km of life. Multiply by 100 to convert to cents."},
{"q":"«The company requires a 15% return on capital employed. What percentage surcharge should be added to its costs?»","ans":[[14,"Profit Mark up"]],"exp":"Target profit = capital × 15%. Mark-up = profit × 100 ÷ total costs."},
{"q":"«Over how many working days a year should standing costs be recovered, and what is the cost per day?»","ans":[[14,"Vehicle Utilisation"]],"exp":"The book uses 5 days × 45 weeks = 225 days. Fixed cost per day = annual fixed costs ÷ 225."},
{"q":"«Which of the coach's costs are standing costs and which are running costs?»","ans":[[14,"Standing Costs"],[14,"Terminology"]],"exp":"Fixed: tax, insurance, wages, depreciation and administration (paid even if the vehicle does not move). Variable: fuel, lubricants, tyres and maintenance (they depend on the km)."},
{"q":"«A UK customer wants the quotation in sterling. Convert your costs using the exchange rate given.»","ans":[[14,"Vehicle Utilisation"]],"exp":"If you are given '£1 = €1.10', divide the euros by 1.10; if you are given '€1 = 90p', multiply by 0.90. The international journey example is on p. 16-17."}]},"202":{"title":"Find it in the book 2","type":"buscar","questions":[{"q":"«Outline the steps which must be taken before any of the staff are made redundant, paying attention to legal requirements and deadlines.»","ans":[[3,"Redundancy"]],"exp":"If it is collective: consult the representatives for at least 30 days, notify the Minister, give no notices during the consultation and wait 30 days from the notification. Thresholds: 5, 10, 10% or 30 depending on the size of the workforce."},
{"q":"«Showing all workings, calculate the statutory redundancy payment for each staff group.»","ans":[[3,"Redundancy"]],"exp":"(2 weeks × complete years + 1 week) × weekly pay, capped at €600. 2 years' service is needed. The full calculation is in the case answer, p. 34."},
{"q":"«A driver with 7 years' service is being dismissed. What minimum notice must the company give?»","ans":[[3,"Notice"]],"exp":"With 5 to 10 years, 4 weeks. Table: less than 13 weeks, none; up to 2 years, 1 week; 2-5, 2; 5-10, 4; 10-15, 6; more than 15, 8 weeks."},
{"q":"«A driver is off work sick. What sick pay must the company pay by law?»","ans":[[3,"Statutory Sick Pay"]],"exp":"5 days a year, at 70% of daily pay, with a maximum of €110 a day, after 13 weeks' service and with a medical certificate."},
{"q":"«A driver has been repeatedly late. Can the company dismiss him fairly? Give the fair reasons for dismissal.»","ans":[[3,"Fair Reasons for Dismissal"]],"exp":"Capability or qualifications, conduct, redundancy, legal prohibition and some other substantial reason, following the disciplinary procedure."},
{"q":"«What must the written statement of terms given to a new driver contain, and by when?»","ans":[[3,"Written Particulars of Employment"]],"exp":"5 core terms within the first 5 days (names, address, duration, pay and hours) and the rest of the terms within a month."},
{"q":"«Explain SEVEN measures you could adopt to make sure the drivers are undertaking their walkround checks correctly.»","ans":[[13,"Daily Walkround Checks and First Use Inspections"]],"exp":"A check before each use and by each driver, a check sheet with the 'Nil' system, training, time in the shift ('other work'), random checks, reports to a responsible person and action if they are not done."},
{"q":"«Design a driver's defect report form for the company.»","ans":[[13,"Defect Reports and Rectification"],[13,"Documentation"]],"exp":"Date, vehicle, driver, km, list of items, defects, driver's signature, repair carried out and signature of the person who did it, and a 'NIL' box. There is a model in the appendix on p. 11."},
{"q":"«How often should the company's coaches receive a safety inspection?»","ans":[[13,"Safety Inspections"]],"exp":"According to the RSA guide, by time (weeks), not by km; vehicles over 12 years old, at most every 6 weeks."},
{"q":"«List the key components of a planned preventive maintenance system.»","ans":[[13,"Key Components of a Planned Maintenance System"]],"exp":"Daily check, scheduled safety inspections, defect reports and repairs, servicing according to the manufacturer, CVRT preparation and audits."},
{"q":"«The company is considering maintaining its fleet in-house. Give the advantages.»","ans":[[13,"In-House Maintenance – Advantages"]],"exp":"More control and flexibility, better cost control, repairs without waiting and own decisions on parts. Disadvantages: space, investment and staff."},
{"q":"«Complete the table showing the minimum licence category and minimum age for each vehicle the company operates.»","ans":[[21,"Categories of Entitlement"],[21,"Minimum Ages"]],"exp":"Up to 8 seats, B; 9 to 16, D1; more than 16, D; with a trailer over 750 kg, +E. Ages: D1, 21 (18 with CPC); D, 24 (21 with CPC, 18 on routes up to 50 km)."},
{"q":"«What is the minimum age for a driver with a Driver CPC to drive a 53-seat coach on tours around Ireland?»","ans":[[21,"Minimum Ages"]],"exp":"21 with the CPC. It only drops to 18 on regular services up to 50 km."},
{"q":"«A driver aged 50 is renewing his Category D entitlement. What medical evidence is required?»","ans":[[21,"Medical Issues"],[21,"Duration of Vocational Licences"]],"exp":"From age 45, a medical check with form D4; under 45, a self-declaration is enough; over 65, renewal every year with a check."},
{"q":"«A Ukrainian national will drive one of your coaches to France. What document must he carry?»","ans":[[21,"International Issues"],[21,"Foreign Drivers"]],"exp":"The Department of Transport's 'driver attestation' (up to 5 years). A letter from the company authorising them to drive the vehicle is also recommended."},
{"q":"«What must the employer check regarding its drivers' licences, and what is the risk if it does not?»","ans":[[21,"Employers' Responsibilities"]],"exp":"It is an offence to let someone drive without the correct licence: licences must be checked when hiring and regularly afterwards."},
{"q":"«Showing the formula, calculate how many buses are required to operate the service.»","ans":[[30,"Calculating the Number of Vehicles"]],"exp":"(Journey time + layover) × 2 ÷ headway, rounded up. On a circular route: (round trip + layover) ÷ headway."},
{"q":"«How many drivers are needed to cover all the duties, including holiday cover?»","ans":[[30,"Crew Rosters or Rotas"]],"exp":"Drivers = shifts in the week ÷ days each one works. Holidays = drivers × holiday weeks ÷ (52 − holiday weeks). Example on p. 8."},
{"q":"«Explain the terms headway, layover and recovery time.»","ans":[[30,"Terminology"]],"exp":"Headway: minutes between buses. Layover: stop at the terminus. Recovery: margin for delays. Frequency = 60 ÷ headway."},
{"q":"«Explain why one of these excursions is subject to the Package Travel Regulations and the other is not.»","ans":[[30,"Package Travel"],[32,"Packaged Travel"]],"exp":"It is a 'package' if at least 2 elements are combined (transport, accommodation or another significant tourist service), at an inclusive price and for more than 24 h or including a night."},
{"q":"«Using all the relevant information in the scenario, draw an organisation chart for the company.»","ans":[[8,"Organisation Charts"]],"exp":"Include all the staff in the case, with their job and the lines of command and communication; support staff go as a horizontal branch. There is a model on p. 3."},
{"q":"«Suggest KPIs the company could use to monitor fuel economy and safety.»","ans":[[8,"Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)"]],"exp":"Consumption per driver, cost per km and accidents per 100,000 km, with targets and regular measurement. Fuel is the biggest cost: every 1% saved counts."},
{"q":"«Identify SEVEN legal requirements for drivers using analogue tachographs, as part of an information sheet.»","ans":[[12,"Responsibilities of the Driver"]],"exp":"Use the correct chart, fill in the centre, carry the charts for the last days, make manual records when not driving or if there is a breakdown, do not use a chart for more than 24 h, return them within 42 days, etc."},
{"q":"«The RSA criticised the company's control of tachograph charts. How will you improve the issue, checking and storage of charts?»","ans":[[12,"Responsibilities of the Operator"]],"exp":"Provide enough charts, get them back within 42 days, keep them for 12 months (2 years for working time), check them and record the checks, and take action with drivers."},
{"q":"«What details must the driver write in the centre field of the chart at the start and at the end of the day?»","ans":[[12,"Centre field"]],"exp":"At the start: name, place and date, registration number and starting km. At the end: place and date of finishing and final km."}]},"203":{"title":"Find it in the book 3","type":"buscar","questions":[{"q":"«The company wants to register two local bus services. To whom must it apply, and what time limits apply?»","ans":[[28,"Local Services in Ireland"],[28,"Changes to ROI Service Registrations"]],"exp":"To the NTA, applying for the Public or Commercial Bus Passenger Service Licence, as well as holding the operator licence (sections 1.4 and 1.6.1, p. 2-3). The time limits are in the answer to case 2, at the end of the module."},
{"q":"«Identify EIGHT of the prescribed particulars which must accompany the application to register the services.»","ans":[[28,"Changes to ROI Service Registrations"]],"exp":"The list is in section 1.6.1 (p. 3-4): the operator's name, address and licence number; type and category of service; the full route with departure, destination and all stops; and it continues on p. 4."},
{"q":"«What document must be carried for a private coach tour around France for 5 days?»","ans":[[28,"Occasional International Services"],[26,"Vehicle Documentation"]],"exp":"It is an occasional service: Journey Form (waybill) filled in before departure, as well as the certified copy of the Community Licence."},
{"q":"«Students are taken to a college event in Belgium under a written contract with the college. What type of service is this, and what must be carried?»","ans":[[28,"Special Regular International Services"]],"exp":"Special regular service: no authorisation is needed if there is a contract between the organiser and the carrier. On board, a copy of the contract."},
{"q":"«While in France, the coach is asked to carry passengers between two French cities. Is this allowed?»","ans":[[28,"Cabotage"]],"exp":"It is cabotage: allowed temporarily as part of an international service, with the copy of the Community Licence and the Journey Form."},
{"q":"«Describe SIX key rules which must be followed to introduce a multi-operator ticketing scheme legally.»","ans":[[29,"Public Transport Regulation and Competition"],[29,"Competition Law – Agreements and Collusion (Section 4)"]],"exp":"Voluntary and non-exclusive, without fixing each operator's prices, a clear revenue split, no exchange of sensitive information, a real benefit for the passenger and open to other operators."},
{"q":"«Two bus operators agree verbally to share out the tourist routes in Munster. Is this legal?»","ans":[[29,"Competition Law – Agreements and Collusion (Section 4)"]],"exp":"No: sharing markets is a cartel prohibited by section 4 and Article 101, even if it is a verbal agreement."},
{"q":"«Calculate the costs, revenue and minimum (net) subsidy for operating the tendered route.»","ans":[[31,"Tendering"]],"exp":"Passengers per journey = seats × occupancy; revenue = passengers × journeys × days × fare; subsidy = (costs + profit) − revenue. There is an example in the case answer, p. 15."},
{"q":"«Which fare system charges the same fare whatever the distance travelled?»","ans":[[31,"graduated · flat rate · zonal"],[31,"Types of Fares"]],"exp":"Flat rate: the same fare whatever the distance. Graduated (or tapered): it rises with distance, but less and less per km. Zonal: by zones."},
{"q":"«Passengers with free travel passes use the service. How is the operator paid for them?»","ans":[[31,"concessionary"]],"exp":"They are concessionary fares: the Free Travel Scheme is paid to the operator by the Department of Social Protection."},
{"q":"«Outline the Package Travel rules on the security of passengers' payments and on limits to price changes.»","ans":[[32,"Packaged Travel"],[30,"Package Travel"]],"exp":"Protection: trust fund, insurance or bond. Price: only if the contract allows it, more than 2% and never in the 30 days before departure."},
{"q":"«Which ferry routes could be used to take a coach from Ireland to France?»","ans":[[32,"Principal Irish Ferry Routes"]],"exp":"The ferry routes from Ireland are in the list on p. 2 (for example, Cork–Roscoff)."},
{"q":"«What passenger list must be compiled before the ferry crossing, and what must it contain?»","ans":[[32,"SOLAS Convention"],[26,"Passenger Documentation"]],"exp":"SOLAS Convention / Directive 98/41: surname, first name, sex, age and special needs, on crossings of more than 20 miles."},
{"q":"«List the documents the driver must carry on an international trip to Germany.»","ans":[[26,"Driver Documentation"]],"exp":"Passport, licence, CPC card (DQC), European health insurance card, German minimum wage document, attestation letter if needed and tachograph records."},
{"q":"«List the documents that must be carried on the coach on an international journey.»","ans":[[26,"Vehicle Documentation"]],"exp":"Original registration document, CVRT certificate, insurance (Green Card recommended), Tempo/Vitesse 100 if needed, certified copies of the licence and the Community Licence, and the Journey Form or authorisation."},
{"q":"«Recommend the most suitable vehicle type for this new local route, taking account of the route, stops, capacity and accessibility.»","ans":[[25,"Vehicle Configurations"],[25,"Safety Considerations"]],"exp":"Look at the operational factors for buses (5.2): type of route, stops, capacity and accessibility (low floor). Comfort and safety are in section 7.1."},
{"q":"«Identify FIVE environmentally friendly options which could be specified for the new coaches.»","ans":[[25,"Environmental Considerations"]],"exp":"Cleaner Euro engines, hybrids or electric, aerodynamic deflectors, low rolling resistance tyres, telematics for efficient driving, etc."},
{"q":"«Extra equipment has increased the coach's unladen weight. What must the transport manager do about the weight plate?»","ans":[[19,"Plating (Weight Certification)"]],"exp":"Re-weigh the vehicle and certify the weights again (re-plating). Never drive above the weights on the plate."},
{"q":"«When must a new coach have its first CVRT test, and how often after that?»","ans":[[19,"test frequency"],[19,"Commercial Vehicle Roadworthiness Test (CVRT)"],[20,"Inspection frequency"]],"exp":"At 1 year from registration and then every 12 months. With a valid CRW; without it the tax cannot be paid."},
{"q":"«A coach is stopped at the roadside and found to have a dangerous defect. Who may stop it and what can happen?»","ans":[[20,"Roadside inspections"],[20,"Roadside Inspections"],[20,"RSA inspection and enforcement regime"]],"exp":"Only the Garda can stop a moving vehicle; then the Garda and the RSA check it. With a dangerous defect, a Prohibition Notice: it cannot be driven until repaired, and it is recorded against the licence."},
{"q":"«The RSA is coming to inspect the depot. What records will they examine?»","ans":[[20,"Operator premises inspections"],[20,"Premises Inspections"]],"exp":"Maintenance schedule, daily check sheets, defect reports, job cards and each vehicle's history."},
{"q":"«What is the speed limit for a single-deck bus designed to carry standing passengers?»","ans":[[22,"ROI Non-Motorway Speed Limits"],[22,"Ireland Motorway Speed Limits"]],"exp":"65 km/h on all roads. Without standing passengers: 80 km/h on ordinary roads and 100 on motorways; with a trailer, 80."},
{"q":"«Which countries require drivers to carry a spare pair of glasses, or a copy of their medical prescriptions?»","ans":[[22,"Requirements in Specific Countries"]],"exp":"Spare glasses: Spain and Germany. Copy of the prescription: France. Also vests, triangles, chains and low emission zones."},
{"q":"«What annual motor tax is payable on a 53-seat coach?»","ans":[[15,"Passenger Vehicle Taxation — Key Points / Bus Motor Tax Rates"],[15,"Motor tax (road tax) requirements"]],"exp":"Buses pay according to seats: 41 to 60 seats, €349–€403 a year. It can be paid for 3, 6 or 12 months."},
{"q":"«What is the maximum permitted length of an articulated bus?»","ans":[[18,"Maximum Lengths"]],"exp":"18.75 m. 2-axle bus, 13.5 m; more than 2 axles, 15 m."}]},"204":{"title":"Find it in the book 4","type":"buscar","questions":[{"q":"«Outline two possible advantages of operating as a sole trader.»","ans":[[2,"Sole Traders"]],"exp":"Easy and cheap to start, full control, keeps all the profit and less paperwork. Disadvantage: unlimited liability."},
{"q":"«Outline two possible advantages of operating as a private limited company.»","ans":[[2,"private limited company"],[2,"Limited Companies"]],"exp":"Limited liability, the company continues even if the owners change, easier to get finance and a more serious image."},
{"q":"«Identify three documents which must be sent to the Companies Registration Office to form the company.»","ans":[[2,"Company Formation and Registration"]],"exp":"The company's constitution, a form with the directors, secretary and registered office, and the required declarations. Details in section 6.2 (p. 7)."},
{"q":"«What are the main duties of the company secretary?»","ans":[[2,"Company (Secretary) — primary duties"]],"exp":"Keeping the company's registers, filing documents with the CRO, organising meetings and writing the minutes."},
{"q":"«Identify and describe the key stages of the risk assessment for this activity.»","ans":[[4,"Risk Assessment"]],"exp":"Identify the hazards, decide who might be harmed and how, assess the risk and decide on the measures, record and implement them, and review them."},
{"q":"«What must the company's safety statement contain?»","ans":[[4,"Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005 (SHWWA)"]],"exp":"The hazards and risks identified, the protective measures, the people responsible and the emergency plans. It is in section 2.4 (p. 3), under the 2005 Act."},
{"q":"«An HSA inspector finds a dangerous activity in the depot. What notices can be issued?»","ans":[[4,"Improvement / Prohibition notices, penalties"],[4,"Health and Safety Authority (HSA)"]],"exp":"Improvement Notice (correct within a deadline) or Prohibition Notice (stop the activity), as well as penalties."},
{"q":"«Calculate the current ratio and the acid test ratio from the balance sheet.»","ans":[[5,"Balance Sheet Terms and Ratios"]],"exp":"Current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities (2:1). Acid test = (current assets − stock) ÷ current liabilities (1:1)."},
{"q":"«Calculate the gross profit and the net profit from the figures given.»","ans":[[5,"Profit and Loss Account"],[5,"Trading Account"]],"exp":"Gross = sales − direct costs. Net = gross − overheads."},
{"q":"«Prepare a cash budget for the next three months.»","ans":[[5,"Cash Flow"]],"exp":"Closing balance = opening balance + receipts − payments, and one month's closing balance is the next month's opening balance. Watch out for customers who pay a month later."},
{"q":"«The company sells its unpaid invoices to a finance company to get cash now. What is this called?»","ans":[[5,"Debt Factoring"]],"exp":"Debt factoring: the finance company advances the money from the invoices in exchange for a fee."},
{"q":"«Complete a table comparing payment methods by cost and convenience, and recommend one.»","ans":[[6,"Methods of Payment"]],"exp":"Cash, cheque, cards, transfer and direct debit: compare cost, speed and security, and recommend one with the reason."},
{"q":"«A customer was overcharged on an invoice. Which document corrects it?»","ans":[[6,"Credit and Debit Notes"]],"exp":"A credit note (returns what was overcharged). A debit note corrects an undercharge."},
{"q":"«For three international jobs, explain how VAT should be handled on the sales invoice.»","ans":[[9,"VAT"]],"exp":"It depends on the place of supply and whether the customer is a business; the explanation is in section 4.5 (p. 8-9)."},
{"q":"«What rate of corporation tax applies to the company's trading profits, and when must it be paid?»","ans":[[9,"Corporation Tax"]],"exp":"12.5% on trading (25% on other income), paid by the 23rd day of the ninth month after the end of the accounting period."},
{"q":"«Which tolls will the coach pay on the M50 and the Dublin Port Tunnel?»","ans":[[9,"Tolls in Ireland"]],"exp":"The M50 has a barrier-free toll (eFlow): it must be paid on time or surcharges follow. The Port Tunnel and other tolls are on p. 11."},
{"q":"«Ace sub-contracts a trip to Ridgeway, who fails to turn up. Who is liable to the customer?»","ans":[[16,"Sub-contracting"]],"exp":"Ace, the original contractor: it remains answerable to the customer, and can then claim against Ridgeway."},
{"q":"«Under the PSV conduct rules, what may the driver do about a drunk and abusive passenger?»","ans":[[16,"PSV Conduct Rules"]],"exp":"Refuse to carry them or ask them to get off; the Garda can help remove them. The driver must be courteous and must not smoke."},
{"q":"«What legal requirements apply to the driver and passenger data the company holds?»","ans":[[17,"Data Protection"]],"exp":"GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018: the company is the Data Controller; data kept secure, used only for its purpose and for as long as needed; access requests answered within 1 month."},
{"q":"«How can vehicle telematics help the company reduce fuel use and maintenance costs?»","ans":[[17,"The Vehicle"]],"exp":"Monitoring of consumption and driving style, remote diagnostics, GPS and maintenance planning."},
{"q":"«Write a set of instructions for drivers setting out their legal responsibilities at, and after, a road traffic accident.»","ans":[[23,"Procedure at the Scene"],[23,"Accident Reports"]],"exp":"Stop; give name, address and vehicle details to anyone with reason to ask; report to the Garda; if anyone is injured, call 112 or 999, do not move them unless in danger, and show the insurance certificate; and fill in the report. Failing to stop or report: up to €20,000 and 10 years in prison."},
{"q":"«The coach hits a parked car and nobody is injured. Is this an 'accident' that must be dealt with under the rules?»","ans":[[23,"Definition of an Accident"]],"exp":"Yes. Under section 106 of the Road Traffic Act 1961, there is an accident if another vehicle or property beside the road is damaged, or if another person or certain animals (horse, cattle, sheep, pig, goat, ass, mule or dog) are injured. Hitting a parked car counts."},
{"q":"«Identify TWO types of insurance the company is legally obliged to take out, and TWO liabilities covered by each.»","ans":[[24,"Third Party Motor Insurance"],[24,"Other Non-Motor Insurances"]],"exp":"The vehicle's third-party insurance (damage and injury to others, passengers included). Employer's liability and public liability insurance are on p. 10-11."},
{"q":"«What evidence of motor insurance should the coach carry on a journey outside the EU?»","ans":[[24,"Green Card"]],"exp":"The Green Card: international proof of the vehicle's insurance."},
{"q":"«A mechanic is injured in the depot and makes a claim. Which insurance covers it?»","ans":[[24,"Other Non-Motor Insurances"]],"exp":"Employer's Liability insurance. Damage to third parties on the premises is Public Liability."}]},"205":{"title":"Find it in the book 5","type":"buscar","questions":[{"q":"«Explain what is meant by 'good repute', and identify the 'relevant persons' who must satisfy it.»","ans":[[10,"Transport Operator Licensing Requirements / Criteria (good repute, financial standing, competence, establishment)"]],"exp":"No serious convictions (or serious transport offences), up to date with Revenue (tax clearance) and passing the Garda check. Relevant persons: the company, its directors, the transport manager and, where applicable, partners or the sole trader. The details of convictions are on p. 8."},
{"q":"«The coach must travel 495 km at an average speed of 55 km/h. Calculate the driving time and say where the breaks must be taken.»","ans":[[11,"EU Regulation 561/2006"],[11,"max driving before a break · breaks"]],"exp":"495 ÷ 55 = 9 h of driving: exactly the normal daily maximum. A 45 min break (or 15 + 30) is needed at the latest after 4 h 30 of driving. Write out the schedule hour by hour in the exam."},
{"q":"«A new coach costs €280,000 including tyres worth €4,000. It will be kept for 6 years and sold for €36,000. Calculate the annual straight-line depreciation.»","ans":[[14,"Straight Line Depreciation"]],"exp":"(280,000 − 4,000 − 36,000) ÷ 6 = 240,000 ÷ 6 = €40,000 a year. The tyres are subtracted because they are a variable cost."},
{"q":"«A driver earning €180 a day, with 2 years' service, is off sick for 5 days. Calculate the statutory sick pay.»","ans":[[3,"Statutory Sick Pay"]],"exp":"70% of 180 = €126, but the maximum is €110 a day: 5 × 110 = €550. Requirements: 13 weeks' service and a medical certificate."},
{"q":"«The company intends to contract out the maintenance of its fleet. What should the maintenance contract include?»","ans":[[13,"Contracted Out Maintenance"]],"exp":"Inspection dates and intervals, what is checked, who repairs defects, deadlines, the records the workshop provides and that the operator remains legally responsible for the vehicle's safety."},
{"q":"«How long is a Category D driving licence valid, and what must a driver do to renew it?»","ans":[[21,"Duration of Vocational Licences"]],"exp":"The professional licence lasts 5 years. To renew it: a medical declaration or a doctor's report depending on age (p. 6), and an up-to-date Driver CPC to work."},
{"q":"«Running time is 50 minutes each way, layover is 10 minutes at one terminus and the headway is 20 minutes. Showing the formula, calculate the number of buses required.»","ans":[[30,"Calculating the Number of Vehicles"]],"exp":"(50 + 10) × 2 ÷ 20 = 120 ÷ 20 = 6 buses. If it does not come out exact, always round up."},
{"q":"«The fleet runs 900,000 km a year at 3 km per litre, with diesel at €1.50 a litre. How much would a 2% improvement in fuel economy save?»","ans":[[8,"Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)"]],"exp":"900,000 ÷ 3 = 300,000 litres × €1.50 = €450,000 a year. 2% less = €9,000 saved."},
{"q":"«Explain the operator's legal responsibilities for the issue, return and keeping of tachograph charts.»","ans":[[12,"Responsibilities of the Operator"]],"exp":"Provide enough charts of the right type, collect them within 42 days at most, check them, keep them for 12 months (2 years if used for working time) and show them to the inspectors within 10 days if requested."},
{"q":"«The company has agreed a contract with a school. What are the essential elements of a valid contract?»","ans":[[2,"Contract Law"]],"exp":"Offer and acceptance, consideration, intention to create legal relations, capacity of the parties and a legal purpose."},
{"q":"«Outline the duties of the employees under the Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005.»","ans":[[4,"Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005 (SHWWA)"]],"exp":"Take care of their own safety and that of others, follow the rules, not work under the influence of alcohol or drugs, use protective equipment, report hazards and defects, and attend training."},
{"q":"«From the balance sheet: current assets €180,000 (including stock of €40,000) and current liabilities €120,000. Calculate the current ratio and the acid test ratio.»","ans":[[5,"Balance Sheet Terms and Ratios"]],"exp":"Current: 180,000 ÷ 120,000 = 1.5 : 1 (ideally 2 : 1). Acid test: (180,000 − 40,000) ÷ 120,000 = 1.17 : 1 (ideally 1 : 1)."},
{"q":"«The fuel supplier wants to be paid by direct debit. Explain how this differs from a standing order.»","ans":[[6,"Methods of Payment"]],"exp":"Standing order: the customer tells their bank to pay a fixed amount on fixed dates and controls it themselves. Direct debit: the supplier, with your authorisation, collects amounts that can vary."},
{"q":"«The company made trading profits of €160,000. Calculate the corporation tax and say when it must be paid.»","ans":[[9,"Corporation Tax"]],"exp":"12.5% of 160,000 = €20,000. It is paid at the latest on the 23rd day of the ninth month after the end of the accounting period."},
{"q":"«On what basis is motor tax charged on buses and coaches, and for what periods can it be paid?»","ans":[[15,"Passenger Vehicle Taxation — Key Points / Bus Motor Tax Rates"]],"exp":"According to the number of seats (not the weight, as for lorries). It can be paid for 3, 6 or 12 months. The rates by seat band are on the same page."},
{"q":"«Draft the main points the company's Conditions of Carriage should cover, and explain how they become binding on passengers.»","ans":[[16,"Conditions of Carriage"]],"exp":"Tickets, luggage, delays and cancellations, refunds, conduct and liability. They are binding if brought to the customer's attention before the contract (on the ticket, the website or at the office). They cannot exclude liability for death or injury."},
{"q":"«A driver asks to see all the personal data the company holds about him. What must the company do, and within what time?»","ans":[[17,"Data Protection"]],"exp":"It is an access request (GDPR): they must be given a copy of their data within 1 month, normally free of charge."},
{"q":"«What is the maximum permitted width of a coach?»","ans":[[18,"Maximum Width"]],"exp":"2.55 metres."},
{"q":"«What is a Certificate of Roadworthiness, and how is it obtained?»","ans":[[19,"Certificate of Roadworthiness (CRW)"]],"exp":"It is issued by the commercial vehicle test centre (CVRT) when the vehicle passes. It is needed to pay motor tax and the disc is displayed on the windscreen."},
{"q":"«One of the coaches is stopped for a roadside inspection. What will be checked, and what can happen?»","ans":[[20,"Roadside inspections"],[20,"Roadside Inspections"]],"exp":"The RSA with the Garda check the condition of the vehicle, the tachograph chart or card, licences and documents. If there are serious defects they can prohibit it from continuing (Prohibition Notice) and fine or prosecute."},
{"q":"«What is the maximum speed of a coach (not carrying standing passengers) on an Irish motorway, and at what speed must its speed limiter be set?»","ans":[[22,"Ireland Motorway Speed Limits"],[22,"Speed Limiters"]],"exp":"100 km/h on the motorway, and the limiter is set at 100 km/h. If it carries standing passengers: 65 km/h."},
{"q":"«The coach is involved in a minor collision in France with a French car. What document should the driver complete with the other driver?»","ans":[[23,"European Accident Statement"]],"exp":"The European accident statement: both drivers write and sign the facts, each keeps a copy and sends it to their insurer. There is no need to admit fault."},
{"q":"«What is covered by third-party motor insurance, and why is it compulsory?»","ans":[[24,"Third Party Motor Insurance"]],"exp":"It covers death, injury and damage caused to other people, passengers included. It is compulsory by law to drive on a public road."},
{"q":"«The company will buy new coaches. Recommend the safety features you would specify.»","ans":[[25,"Safety Considerations"]],"exp":"ABS/EBS brakes and retarder, seatbelts, speed limiter, cameras and sensors, lane departure warning, automatic emergency braking, emergency exits and fire extinguishers."},
{"q":"«A group will travel on a coach tour to Morocco. Which documents must the passengers carry?»","ans":[[26,"Passenger Documentation"]],"exp":"A current passport (with enough validity left), a visa if the country requires it, and travel insurance is recommended. The European health insurance card is not valid outside the EU."},
{"q":"«Explain what an international shuttle service is.»","ans":[[28,"International Shuttle Services"]],"exp":"Repeated outward and return journeys carrying groups formed in advance from the same origin to the same destination; each group returns to the origin on a later journey."},
{"q":"«What powers does the Competition and Consumer Protection Commission have?»","ans":[[29,"Competition and Consumer Protection Commission (CCPC)"]],"exp":"Investigate, carry out surprise searches of offices (dawn raids), demand documents, impose administrative fines (with High Court approval), issue orders and send serious cartels to the prosecutor (DPP)."},
{"q":"«A single fare of €12 is charged for a journey of 48 km. What is the fare per kilometre?»","ans":[[31,"Types of Fares"]],"exp":"12 ÷ 48 = €0.25 per km (25 cents)."},
{"q":"«The coach tour goes from France to Italy across the Alps. Which tunnels could it use?»","ans":[[32,"Tunnels"]],"exp":"The Fréjus tunnel and the Mont Blanc tunnel connect France and Italy. Height, weight and toll restrictions must be checked beforehand."},
{"q":"«The company wants a licence for 6 coaches. Calculate the financial standing it must show.»","ans":[[10,"Transport Operator Licensing Requirements / Criteria (good repute, financial standing, competence, establishment)"]],"exp":"€9,000 for the first + €5,000 for each of the other 5 = 9,000 + 25,000 = €34,000."}]},"206":{"title":"Find it in the book 6","type":"buscar","questions":[{"q":"«Explain the requirement for a 'stable and effective establishment' in Ireland.»","ans":[[10,"Transport Operator Licensing Requirements / Criteria (good repute, financial standing, competence, establishment)"]],"exp":"Real premises in Ireland (not just a letterbox) where the main documents are kept (accounts, personnel, driving and rest times, maintenance), and at least one vehicle registered, insured and taxed, with suitable premises to operate from."},
{"q":"«A driver drove 50 hours last week. What is the maximum he may drive this week?»","ans":[[11,"daily · weekly · fortnightly driving"]],"exp":"40 hours: the maximum for two consecutive weeks is 90 h (90 − 50 = 40), even though the maximum for one week is 56 h."},
{"q":"«Diesel costs €1.40 a litre before VAT and the coach does 3.5 km per litre. Calculate the fuel cost per kilometre.»","ans":[[14,"Vehicle Running Costs"]],"exp":"1.40 ÷ 3.5 = €0.40 per km (40 cents). The price without VAT is used because the company recovers the VAT."},
{"q":"«A driver with 12 years' service is being dismissed. What is the minimum statutory notice?»","ans":[[3,"Notice"]],"exp":"6 weeks (10 to 15 years' service). Table: less than 13 weeks, none; up to 2 years, 1; 2 to 5, 2; 5 to 10, 4; 10 to 15, 6; more than 15, 8 weeks."},
{"q":"«What records should be kept in each vehicle's maintenance file, and for how long?»","ans":[[13,"Documentation"]],"exp":"Safety inspection sheets, drivers' defect reports and their repair, workshop job cards, CVRT results and calibrations. They are kept for at least 15 months."},
{"q":"«A driver passed his Category B test in 1995. What passenger vehicles may he drive on that licence?»","ans":[[21,"Preserved Entitlements"]],"exp":"Anyone who got their B licence before 1 January 1997 keeps a limited D1 (minibus with 9 to 16 seats), with code 101: they cannot charge (not for hire or reward). It is not valid for working for the company."},
{"q":"«A circular route takes 85 minutes to complete, plus 10 minutes' layover, and the headway is 15 minutes. How many buses are needed?»","ans":[[30,"Calculating the Number of Vehicles"]],"exp":"On a circular route you do not multiply by 2: (85 + 10) ÷ 15 = 6.33 → 7 buses (always round up)."},
{"q":"«The directors want to remove a layer of management. Outline the advantages and disadvantages of a flat hierarchy.»","ans":[[8,"Flat Hierarchies"]],"exp":"Advantages: better communication from top to bottom, more delegation, lower costs and faster response. Disadvantages: each manager supervises many people and there are fewer promotions."},
{"q":"«A driver returns from 3 weeks' sick leave and has no tachograph records for that period. What document should he carry?»","ans":[[12,"Letter of Attestation"]],"exp":"The letter of attestation of activities signed by the company, explaining that they were on sick leave or on holiday on those days."},
{"q":"«Two friends plan to run the business as a partnership. Outline the disadvantages of a partnership.»","ans":[[2,"Partnerships"]],"exp":"Unlimited liability: each partner is liable with their own assets for all the debts, including the other partner's. Also, disputes between partners, and the partnership can be dissolved if one dies or leaves."},
{"q":"«Drivers working in the depot workshop are exposed to high noise levels. What does the law require at 80 dB(A) and at 85 dB(A)?»","ans":[[4,"…(General Application) Regulations (paragraph)"]],"exp":"At 80 dB(A): provide hearing protection, information and training. At 85 dB(A): wearing it is compulsory and the area must be signposted. The maximum limit is 87 dB(A). Details on p. 8."},
{"q":"«The company's profit before tax was €60,000. Total assets are €620,000 and current liabilities €120,000. Calculate the return on capital employed.»","ans":[[5,"Balance Sheet Terms and Ratios"]],"exp":"Capital employed = 620,000 − 120,000 = 500,000. ROCE = 60,000 × 100 ÷ 500,000 = 12%."},
{"q":"«List, in order, the documents used in a commercial transaction from the first enquiry to the customer's payment.»","ans":[[6,"Documents Used in Commercial Transactions"]],"exp":"Quotation, order, invoice, credit or debit notes if needed, statement of account and the customer's remittance advice."},
{"q":"«What are the employer's obligations for PAYE, PRSI and USC on the drivers' wages?»","ans":[[9,"Employers' Payments"]],"exp":"Deduct PAYE, USC and PRSI from each payroll, report it to Revenue on or before the pay date (Real Time Reporting) and pay Revenue what was deducted together with the employer's PRSI."},
{"q":"«Two coaches will be off the road from November to March. What should the company do about motor tax?»","ans":[[15,"…Non-Use Declaration (RF111A)"],[15,"Off-Road Declaration"]],"exp":"Submit a declaration of non-use in advance: that way no tax is paid for those months, but the vehicle cannot be driven or parked on a public road."},
{"q":"«The company's booking agent agreed a job that was outside his authority. Is the company bound by it?»","ans":[[16,"Agents"]],"exp":"No: the company (the principal) is not bound by what the agent did without authority; the customer can claim against the agent."},
{"q":"«How can technology improve the information given to passengers?»","ans":[[17,"Passengers"]],"exp":"Real-time information at stops and in apps (with GPS), online and smart-card ticket sales, and open timetable data for journey planners."},
{"q":"«What are the maximum permitted lengths of a two-axle coach, a coach with more than two axles, and an articulated bus?»","ans":[[18,"Maximum Lengths"]],"exp":"2-axle rigid: 13.5 m. Rigid with more than 2 axles: 15 m. Articulated: 18.75 m."},
{"q":"«A coach has been converted and an extra axle fitted. What must be done before it is used?»","ans":[[19,"Plating (Weight Certification)"]],"exp":"Have the weights certified again (re-plating): a new plate with the maximum authorised weights. It is needed when importing or when modifying the vehicle in a way that changes its weights or axles."},
{"q":"«What penalties can follow if a coach is used in a dangerous condition or without a valid CRW?»","ans":[[20,"Penalties for non-compliance"],[20,"Legal Liability and Penalties"]],"exp":"Fines, penalty points, immobilisation or a ban on driving, and prosecution. It can also damage the operator's good repute and its licence."},
{"q":"«Where may the company's coaches not be parked on the road at night?»","ans":[[22,"Parking on the Road at Night"]],"exp":"Large passenger vehicles are very restricted from parking at night on public roads in built-up areas: they must be left at the depot or in permitted places."},
{"q":"«A passenger falls and is injured when the driver brakes sharply. What must the driver do?»","ans":[[23,"Procedure at the Scene"],[23,"Accident Reports"]],"exp":"Stop, look after the injured person and call for help, tell the Garda (someone is injured), give their details, note witnesses and facts, and inform the company, which notifies the insurer."},
{"q":"«What insurance could protect the drivers if they are injured or fall ill while working abroad?»","ans":[[24,"Crew Member Insurance"]],"exp":"Insurance for the crew: personal accident, and medical and repatriation costs abroad."},
{"q":"«Apart from the purchase price, what economic factors should be considered when choosing a new coach?»","ans":[[25,"Economic Considerations"]],"exp":"The cost over its whole life: consumption, maintenance and parts, tyres, insurance and tax, resale value, finance and reliability."},
{"q":"«The company wants to run a scheduled coach service from Dublin to London. To whom must it apply, and how long does the decision take?»","ans":[[28,"Regular International Services"]],"exp":"Apply for the authorisation to the authority of the country where the route starts (in Ireland, the NTA), which consults the other countries involved. The decision must be taken within 4 months. The authorisation must be carried on board."},
{"q":"«The largest operator in the area cuts its fares below cost to drive a new competitor off a route. Is this legal?»","ans":[[29,"Abuse of a Dominant Position"]],"exp":"No: if it has a dominant position it is abuse (predatory pricing): prices below cost with the intention of driving out the competitor."},
{"q":"«Explain how a zonal fare system works.»","ans":[[31,"graduated · flat rate · zonal"]],"exp":"The service area is divided into zones: within a zone a fixed price is paid and the price rises when crossing into other zones. In Ireland the zones are set by the NTA."},
{"q":"«Passengers are returning from a tour outside the EU. Explain the green and red customs channels.»","ans":[[32,"Customs"]],"exp":"Green: nothing to declare. Red: carrying goods that pay duty or are restricted, and they must be declared."},
{"q":"«May a driver split his regular daily rest period? If so, how?»","ans":[[11,"daily rest · double manned"]],"exp":"Yes: in two parts, first at least 3 hours and then at least 9 hours, 12 h in total."},
{"q":"«The coach has 6 tyres costing €450 each, with a life of 80,000 km. Calculate the tyre cost per kilometre in cents.»","ans":[[14,"Vehicle Running Costs"]],"exp":"6 × 450 = 2,700 ÷ 80,000 = €0.03375 per km = 3.4 cents per km."}]},"207":{"title":"Find it in the book 7","type":"buscar","questions":[{"q":"«What conditions must the person nominated as transport manager meet?»","ans":[[10,"Two types of transport manager"]],"exp":"Hold the transport manager CPC, be of good repute, reside in the EU and genuinely and continuously manage the transport. If internal, have a real link with the company (employee, director or partner); if external, a contract and a maximum of 4 companies and 50 vehicles."},
{"q":"«What is the maximum average weekly working time for the coach drivers, and over what reference period is it calculated?»","ans":[[11,"Working Time for EU-Regulated Drivers"]],"exp":"An average of 48 hours, normally calculated over 4 months, and never more than 60 hours in one week."},
{"q":"«An excursion costs €2,100 to operate, including profit, and 35 tickets are expected to be sold. What should each ticket cost?»","ans":[[14,"Vehicle Utilisation"]],"exp":"2,100 ÷ 35 = €60 per ticket. Divide by the tickets you expect to sell, not by the seats on the coach."},
{"q":"«A driver aged 30 with 6 years and 3 months' service earns €800 a week. Calculate his statutory redundancy payment.»","ans":[[3,"Redundancy"]],"exp":"Only complete years count: (2 × 6 + 1) = 13 weeks. Pay is capped at €600: 13 × 600 = €7,800."},
{"q":"«How far in advance should the safety inspection and service programme be planned?»","ans":[[13,"Organisation and Planning"]],"exp":"At least 6 months in advance, in a calendar or planner with the dates for each vehicle."},
{"q":"«What periodic training must a professional driver complete to keep his Driver CPC?»","ans":[[21,"Vocational Training and Testing"]],"exp":"35 hours every 5 years, in 5 modules of 7 hours (1 a year is recommended), at an RSA-approved centre."},
{"q":"«The depot has 40 drivers, each entitled to 4 weeks' holiday a year. How many extra drivers are needed for holiday cover?»","ans":[[30,"Crew Rosters or Rotas"]],"exp":"40 × 4 ÷ (52 − 4) = 160 ÷ 48 = 3.33 → 4 drivers (round up)."},
{"q":"«Explain the disadvantages of a tall hierarchy.»","ans":[[8,"Tall Hierarchies"]],"exp":"Poor communication from top to bottom because of so many levels, slow decisions and higher management costs."},
{"q":"«An enforcement officer stops one of your drivers using an analogue tachograph. Which records must he be able to produce?»","ans":[[12,"Responsibilities of the Driver"]],"exp":"The charts for the current day and the previous 28 days, and their driver card if they have one, as well as manual records and certificates for days without activity."},
{"q":"«In what ways can a partnership be dissolved?»","ans":[[2,"dissolved by… (expiry, consent…)"]],"exp":"At the end of the agreed term or project, by agreement or notice of the partners, on the death or bankruptcy of a partner, if the business becomes illegal, or by court order."},
{"q":"«What powers does an inspector of the Health and Safety Authority have when visiting the depot?»","ans":[[4,"Health and Safety Authority (HSA)"]],"exp":"Enter the workplace, inspect, take samples and photos, demand documents and question staff, and issue improvement or prohibition notices. It can also take the employer to court."},
{"q":"«The company plans to buy two new coaches. Suggest suitable sources of finance.»","ans":[[5,"Sources of Funds"]],"exp":"Retained profits, a long-term bank loan, hire purchase, leasing or new shares or partners. An overdraft is not suitable for buying vehicles."},
{"q":"«Apart from a current account, what services could the company's bank provide?»","ans":[[6,"Major Services Provided by High Street Banks"]],"exp":"Deposit account, overdraft, loans, standing orders and direct debits, transfers, night safe, foreign exchange and advice."},
{"q":"«The owner is considering starting as a sole trader. What taxes will he have to pay on his profits, and when?»","ans":[[9,"Self Employment"]],"exp":"Income tax, USC and self-employed PRSI (Class S). The return must be filed and paid every year by 31 October (pay and file)."},
{"q":"«A coach is bought second-hand in Northern Ireland. What must be done to register it in the Republic?»","ans":[[15,"Registering the vehicle (VRT)"]],"exp":"Take it to an NCTS centre for the registration inspection within the legal deadline, present the purchase documents and pay the registration tax (VRT) if it applies."},
{"q":"«What are the advantages to the company of owning its own coaches?»","ans":[[16,"Owned Transport"]],"exp":"Full control of the vehicle and its use, its own image and colours, availability when needed and freedom to decide on maintenance and when to sell it."},
{"q":"«How can telematics be used to monitor and improve the drivers' performance?»","ans":[[17,"The Driver"]],"exp":"It measures each driver's harsh braking and acceleration, revs, idling, speed and consumption; it is used to train them in efficient and safe driving."},
{"q":"«What is the maximum gross weight of a three-axle coach?»","ans":[[18,"Maximum Gross Weights"]],"exp":"25 tonnes (26 t if it meets certain conditions, such as air suspension)."},
{"q":"«Outline the operator's responsibilities regarding plating and testing of the fleet.»","ans":[[19,"Operator Responsibilities"]],"exp":"Make sure each vehicle has its correct weights plate and a valid CRW, book the test in time, do not exceed the weights and keep the vehicle in good condition between tests."},
{"q":"«What does the RSA expect from drivers' daily walk-around checks?»","ans":[[20,"Daily Walk-Around Checks"]],"exp":"That the driver checks the vehicle before using it each day, records defects in writing (also when there are none) and that they are repaired before the vehicle is driven."},
{"q":"«A coach tour will go to France. What equipment must the driver carry there that is not required in Ireland?»","ans":[[22,"Requirements in Specific Countries"]],"exp":"An approved breathalyser (and a copy of the prescription if they take medication), as well as triangles and reflective vests."},
{"q":"«What must be shown on the coach's windscreen to prove it is insured?»","ans":[[24,"Motor Insurance — Other Key Facts"]],"exp":"The insurance disc, next to the tax disc and the CRW disc. The insurance certificate is kept at the company."},
{"q":"«The company will run sightseeing tours in the city centre. Which vehicle configuration would you recommend?»","ans":[[25,"Vehicle Configurations"]],"exp":"A double-decker bus, ideally open-top, low-floor and with wheelchair access."},
{"q":"«A driver will take a coach to Turkey. Which personal documents must he carry?»","ans":[[26,"Driver Documentation"]],"exp":"Passport (and visa if needed), driving licence and International Driving Permit (IDP), CPC card, tachograph card, a letter from the company authorising them to drive the vehicle and an attestation of activities if needed."},
{"q":"«A company takes its own employees to a conference in Belgium in its own bus, driven by its own staff. What document does it need?»","ans":[[28,"International Own Account Services"]],"exp":"An own-account transport certificate: no operator licence is needed, but the transport must be ancillary to its business, without charging, with its own vehicles and drivers."},
{"q":"«Who plans and funds public bus services in Ireland, and does competition law still apply to them?»","ans":[[29,"Public Transport Regulation and Competition"]],"exp":"The NTA plans, licenses commercial routes and awards public service contracts (PSO). Competition law still applies: no agreements between operators and no abuse of their position."},
{"q":"«A route uses a 50-seat bus with an expected load factor of 70%, 8 trips a day for 300 days, at an average fare of €4. Calculate the annual revenue.»","ans":[[31,"Tendering"]],"exp":"Passengers per journey = 50 × 70% = 35. Revenue = 35 × 8 × 300 × 4 = €336,000 a year."},
{"q":"«Explain the penalties the company could face if illegal immigrants are found hidden on one of its coaches, and its possible defences.»","ans":[[32,"Immigration Controls"]],"exp":"Fines of up to €3,000 per person (and prison if it is people trafficking). Defence: having an effective security system, using it properly, and the driver not knowing anything or having reason to suspect."},
{"q":"«A driver drives for 3 hours, takes a 15-minute break, then drives for another 1 hour 30 minutes. What must he do now?»","ans":[[11,"max driving before a break · breaks"]],"exp":"They have reached 4 h 30 of driving with only a 15 min break: now they must stop for at least 30 min (15 + 30 = 45). The order must be 15 first and 30 afterwards."},
{"q":"«A director has been convicted of a serious offence. Within what time must the licence holder inform the Minister?»","ans":[[10,"Transport Operator Licensing Requirements / Criteria (good repute, financial standing, competence, establishment)"]],"exp":"In writing, within 3 months of the end of the appeal period (or of the appeal being decided or withdrawn). Details on p. 8."}]},"208":{"title":"Find it in the book 8","type":"buscar","questions":[{"q":"«Who issues the Road Passenger Transport Operator Licence, for how long is it valid, and can it be transferred to another company?»","ans":[[10,"Overview of Passenger Transport Operator Licensing"]],"exp":"It is issued by the RTOL section of the Department of Transport. It lasts up to 5 years and is personal: it cannot be sold or transferred without the Department's authorisation."},
{"q":"«A coach driver will work 10 hours today. What breaks must he take under the working time rules?»","ans":[[11,"breaks"]],"exp":"More than 9 hours of work: a 45-minute break (6 to 9 hours would be 30 minutes), in parts of at least 15 minutes. No one can work more than 6 hours in a row without a break."},
{"q":"«Administration costs are €60,000 a year. The fleet runs 1,200,000 km and coach C runs 150,000 km. Allocate the administration cost to coach C on a distance basis.»","ans":[[14,"Apportioning Administration Costs – Distance Basis"]],"exp":"60,000 ÷ 1,200,000 × 150,000 = €7,500 for coach C."},
{"q":"«A driver is pregnant. Outline her maternity leave entitlements.»","ans":[[3,"Maternity Rights"]],"exp":"26 weeks of maternity leave plus 16 optional unpaid weeks. The benefit is paid by Social Protection if she has the contributions; the company does not have to pay her salary. She has the right to return to her job and to paid time off for ante-natal visits."},
{"q":"«List the equipment the in-house workshop would need to carry out safety inspections.»","ans":[[13,"In-House Maintenance – Equipment Required"]],"exp":"Pit, ramp or lift, roller brake tester, headlamp aiming equipment, diagnostic and measuring tools, and smoke meter."},
{"q":"«A Garda asks one of your drivers to produce his driving licence, but he does not have it with him. What happens?»","ans":[[21,"Production of a Licence"]],"exp":"The driver must always carry the licence. If they do not have it, they must produce it at the Garda station named within 10 days."},
{"q":"«A bus runs every 12 minutes. How many buses an hour pass each stop in one direction?»","ans":[[30,"Terminology"]],"exp":"60 ÷ 12 = 5 buses an hour."},
{"q":"«What is a work plan, and how should it be drawn up?»","ans":[[8,"Work Plans"]],"exp":"A plan that says what has to be done, by whom, with what resources and by when. It should have demanding but realistic objectives and be followed up with regular reviews."},
{"q":"«The distance trace on an analogue chart shows 6 complete 'V' shapes. How far did the vehicle travel?»","ans":[[12,"Tachograph Charts"]],"exp":"Each stroke is 5 km, so each complete 'V' is 10 km: 6 × 10 = 60 km."},
{"q":"«Outline the main duties of the company's directors.»","ans":[[2,"Directors"]],"exp":"Act in good faith and in the interest of the company, comply with company law, keep proper accounts, avoid conflicts of interest, file the accounts and the annual return with the CRO, and take creditors into account."},
{"q":"«What penalties can be imposed for breaches of the Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005?»","ans":[[4,"Improvement / Prohibition notices, penalties"]],"exp":"Improvement and prohibition notices. In a summary trial (District Court): up to €5,000 and/or 6 months in prison; on indictment: up to €3 million and/or 2 years."},
{"q":"«Net profit rose from €80,000 last year to €92,000 this year. Calculate the percentage increase.»","ans":[[5,"Profit and Loss Account"]],"exp":"(92,000 − 80,000) × 100 ÷ 80,000 = 15%."},
{"q":"«Explain the purpose of a statement of account and of a remittance advice.»","ans":[[6,"Documents Used in Commercial Transactions"]],"exp":"Statement: a monthly summary to the customer with the previous balance, invoices, payments and what is owed. Remittance advice: sent by the customer with the payment to say which invoices it pays."},
{"q":"«What are the penalties for using or keeping an untaxed coach on a public road?»","ans":[[15,"Penalties for Non-Compliance"]],"exp":"It is an offence to use it or keep it parked on a public road without tax: fines, the vehicle can be removed and the arrears must be paid."},
{"q":"«During the summer peak the company may hire in coaches with drivers from other operators. What are the advantages?»","ans":[[16,"Hire of Carriers"]],"exp":"No money tied up in vehicles, the fleet adjusts to demand and there are no driver or maintenance costs. In return, there is less control over the service."},
{"q":"«How can computer systems help the company with bookings, invoicing and accounts?»","ans":[[17,"The Business"]],"exp":"Booking and planning software, invoicing and accounting, electronic data interchange (EDI) with customers and banks, and cloud backups."},
{"q":"«Passengers' luggage is stolen from a coach on tour. What type of insurance could cover the company's risks?»","ans":[[24,"Operational Risks Insurance"]],"exp":"Insurance for business risks: passengers' luggage and belongings, theft, fire on the premises and loss of income from business interruption."},
{"q":"«What security measures should be considered when specifying new coaches?»","ans":[[25,"Security Considerations"]],"exp":"Immobiliser and alarm, GPS tracker, locks on the luggage holds, CCTV cameras and a protected driver's cab."},
{"q":"«Which vehicles may use bus lanes, and how do bus lanes and bus stops affect the company's services?»","ans":[[28,"Traffic Regulation and Bus Operations (Ireland)"]],"exp":"Bus lanes are for buses (and taxis and bicycles, depending on the sign) during the hours shown; they help keep to the timetables. You cannot stop or park in them or at bus stops."},
{"q":"«Which groups of passengers receive concessionary fares in Ireland?»","ans":[[31,"concessionary"]],"exp":"Those with the Free Travel pass and their companion, young adults (Young Adult), students and children."},
{"q":"«What minimum passport validity should your drivers have for trips outside the EU?»","ans":[[32,"Passport and Visa Requirements"]],"exp":"At least 3 months beyond the planned departure date (6 months in some countries in the Middle East, Asia and Africa). Also check whether a visa is needed."},
{"q":"«On a tour in Europe the driver sees a yellow diamond sign with a white border. What does it mean?»","ans":[[22,"International Road Signs"]],"exp":"A priority road: at junctions, vehicles travelling on it have right of way."},
{"q":"«A driver works a local service exempt from the EU rules. What are the domestic limits on driving, spreadover and daily rest?»","ans":[[11,"Domestic Regulations"]],"exp":"A 30 min break after 5 h 30 of driving, 10 h of driving a day, a total working day (spreadover) of 16 h at most and a daily rest of 10 h (8 h 30 three times a week)."},
{"q":"«A UK customer wants the quotation in sterling. The price is €4,500 and the rate is £1 = €1.18. What should be quoted?»","ans":[[14,"Vehicle Utilisation"]],"exp":"If £1 = €1.18, divide: 4,500 ÷ 1.18 = £3,813.56."},
{"q":"«The company wants to employ casual drivers on zero-hours contracts. Is this allowed?»","ans":[[3,"Other General Employee Rights (zero hours, time off…)"]],"exp":"As a general rule they are prohibited. They are only allowed in specific cases: genuinely occasional work, emergencies or short replacements."},
{"q":"«Several of the coaches are 14 years old. What is the maximum interval between their safety inspections?»","ans":[[13,"Safety Inspections"]],"exp":"At most every 6 weeks for vehicles aged 12 years or more. The interval is set in weeks, not km."},
{"q":"«What is the minimum age to drive the company's 16-seat minibuses?»","ans":[[21,"Minimum Ages"]],"exp":"21, or 18 with the Driver CPC for certain work. For D (large coach) it is 24, or 21 with the CPC."},
{"q":"«There are 66 driver duties a week and each driver works 5 days. How many drivers are needed, before holiday cover?»","ans":[[30,"Crew Rosters or Rotas"]],"exp":"66 ÷ 5 = 13.2 → 14 drivers (always round up). Then add the holiday cover."},
{"q":"«The bank balance at the start of the month is €12,000. Receipts will be €45,000 and payments €51,000. What is the closing balance?»","ans":[[5,"Cash Flow"]],"exp":"12,000 + 45,000 − 51,000 = €6,000. That balance is the next month's opening balance."},
{"q":"«The company wants 10 coaches on its licence. Calculate the financial standing required.»","ans":[[10,"Transport Operator Licensing Requirements / Criteria (good repute, financial standing, competence, establishment)"]],"exp":"€9,000 + 9 × €5,000 = 9,000 + 45,000 = €54,000."}]},"209":{"title":"Find it in the book 9","type":"buscar","questions":[{"q":"«Explain the difference between a national and an international Road Passenger Transport Operator Licence, and what the international licence allows.»","ans":[[10,"…International / Community Licence (paragraph)"]],"exp":"The national licence only allows work within Ireland. The international one also allows journeys abroad and gives the Community Licence: one certified copy for each authorised vehicle, carried on board."},
{"q":"«What working time records must be kept for the drivers, for how long, and what limit applies if they do night work?»","ans":[[11,"night work · records"]],"exp":"Working time must be recorded and kept for 2 years. If they work at night, a maximum of 10 hours of work in 24 hours (unless there is a collective agreement)."},
{"q":"«A coach costs €200,000, including tyres of €5,000. Depreciate it at 25% a year on the reducing balance. What is the depreciation in years 1 and 2?»","ans":[[14,"Reducing Balance Depreciation"]],"exp":"Base = 200,000 − 5,000 = 195,000. Year 1: 25% = €48,750 (146,250 left). Year 2: 25% of 146,250 = €36,562.50."},
{"q":"«A dismissed driver believes the dismissal was unfair. To which body should he complain, and within what time?»","ans":[[3,"Organisations (WRC, Labour Court…)"]],"exp":"To the Workplace Relations Commission (WRC) within 6 months of the dismissal (12 if there is reasonable cause). It can then be appealed to the Labour Court."},
{"q":"«A driver reports a defect on his daily check sheet. What procedure should be followed?»","ans":[[13,"Defect Reports and Rectification"]],"exp":"The report goes to a person with authority (for example, the workshop manager), who decides whether the vehicle can go out. It is repaired, what was done, by whom and when is recorded and signed, and it is kept in the vehicle's history."},
{"q":"«How many penalty points lead to disqualification, and why does this matter to the operator?»","ans":[[21,"Conduct and Disciplinary Matters"]],"exp":"12 points in 3 years (7 for learner permit holders): 6 months off the road. The company must check licences, because it cannot let someone drive without a valid licence."},
{"q":"«Running time is 35 minutes each way, layover 5 minutes and headway 10 minutes. How many buses are needed?»","ans":[[30,"Calculating the Number of Vehicles"]],"exp":"(35 + 5) × 2 ÷ 10 = 80 ÷ 10 = 8 buses."},
{"q":"«Draw an organisation chart for the company. What should it show, and what is meant by 'span of control'?»","ans":[[8,"Organisation Charts"]],"exp":"All the jobs, who reports to whom (lines of command) and how they communicate. The 'span of control' is the number of people who report directly to each manager."},
{"q":"«How often must an analogue tachograph be inspected and recalibrated, and what tolerances are allowed?»","ans":[[12,"Technical Aspects of the Analogue Tachograph"]],"exp":"Inspection every 2 years and full calibration every 6 (or when the seals are broken). Tolerances: ±6 km/h for speed, ±4% for distance and ±2 min a day."},
{"q":"«Compare a public limited company (PLC) with a designated activity company (DAC).»","ans":[[2,"public limited company (PLC)"],[2,"designated activity company (DAC)"]],"exp":"PLC: minimum capital of €25,000, can sell shares to the public and its name ends in 'plc'. DAC: private, can only carry out the activities in its constitution, up to 149 members, cannot offer shares to the public and its name ends in 'DAC'."},
{"q":"«Outline the general duties of the employer under the Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005.»","ans":[[4,"Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005 (SHWWA)"]],"exp":"Protect workers' safety and health as far as reasonably practicable: a safe place and equipment, assess the risks, prepare the safety statement, provide training and protective equipment, and have emergency plans."},
{"q":"«Explain the difference between fixed assets and current assets, giving examples for a coach company.»","ans":[[5,"Fixed Assets"],[5,"Current Assets"]],"exp":"Fixed assets: things used for years (depots, coaches, workshop equipment). Current assets: things that turn into money within a year (cash, bank, debtors, stocks of parts and diesel)."},
{"q":"«Apart from motor tax and an operator licence, what else is required to carry passengers for hire or reward in a large bus?»","ans":[[15,"Passenger Vehicles — Additional Requirements"]],"exp":"The large public service vehicle licence (LPSV), insurance for carrying fare-paying passengers, a valid CRW and a driver with a D licence and the CPC."},
{"q":"«What is examined during the CVRT test of a coach?»","ans":[[19,"Commercial Vehicle Roadworthiness Test (CVRT)"],[20,"Scope of the inspection"]],"exp":"Brakes, steering and suspension, wheels and tyres, lights, chassis and bodywork, glass and mirrors, emissions, tachograph and limiter, and the safety equipment."},
{"q":"«A driver is caught speeding. What penalties can apply?»","ans":[[22,"Speeding"]],"exp":"A traffic fine with penalty points or, if it goes to court, higher fines and even disqualification. The company's risk rating goes down."},
{"q":"«Two operators agree to submit identical prices in a school transport tender. Is this legal?»","ans":[[29,"Competition Law – Agreements and Collusion (Section 4)"]],"exp":"No: it is bid-rigging, a cartel prohibited by section 4 of the Competition Act. It is a serious offence even if the agreement is verbal."},
{"q":"«How can fare evasion and driver fraud be reduced on the company's bus services?»","ans":[[31,"Revenue Collection"]],"exp":"Electronic ticket machines, smart cards and cashless payment, sealed safes, conductors and inspectors, and checking the machines' data."},
{"q":"«Is Ireland part of the Schengen area, and what does this mean for a coach tour to France?»","ans":[[32,"The Schengen Agreement"]],"exp":"No: Ireland is not in Schengen (because of the Common Travel Area with the United Kingdom). There are passport checks when leaving and returning; within Schengen there are no longer checks between countries."},
{"q":"«What geographical factors should be considered when planning a route through mountainous areas?»","ans":[[32,"Geographical Considerations"]],"exp":"Gradients and bends (power, gears and retarder), mountain passes closed in winter, snow and chains, tunnels and their height and weight limits, and the fact that the shortest route may be the slowest."},
{"q":"«List THREE passenger operations that are exempt from Regulation 561/2006.»","ans":[[11,"EU Regulation 561/2006"]],"exp":"Regular services up to 50 km, vehicles for a maximum of 9 people including the driver, non-commercial vehicles with 10 to 17 seats, and emergency and armed forces vehicles."},
{"q":"«The company requires a profit of €45,000 on total costs of €300,000. What percentage mark-up should be added?»","ans":[[14,"Profit Mark up"]],"exp":"45,000 × 100 ÷ 300,000 = 15% on costs."},
{"q":"«A driver earning €200 a day is off sick for 3 days. How much statutory sick pay must the company pay?»","ans":[[3,"Statutory Sick Pay"]],"exp":"70% of 200 = €140, but the cap is €110 a day: 3 × 110 = €330."},
{"q":"«What should a periodic safety inspection cover, and who should carry it out?»","ans":[[13,"Safety Inspections"]],"exp":"A full inspection using the RSA list (brakes, steering, suspension, wheels, lights, chassis...). It is done by a technically competent person, who records and signs it; they are not asked to repair while inspecting."},
{"q":"«A driver will tow a luggage trailer of 1,500 kg behind a coach. What licence entitlement does he need?»","ans":[[21,"Categories of Entitlement"]],"exp":"A D+E licence, because the trailer is over 750 kg. With a D licence only trailers up to 750 kg can be towed."},
{"q":"«Explain what a crew roster (rota) is and what it must take into account.»","ans":[[30,"Crew Rosters or Rotas"]],"exp":"The allocation of shifts among drivers. It must respect driving, rest and working time rules, share weekends and bad shifts fairly, and cover holidays and sick leave."},
{"q":"«Some drivers only work on an out-of-scope local service. What records must they keep?»","ans":[[12,"Domestic Records"]],"exp":"A weekly record book (or the approved system): driving, duty and rest hours. The company checks it, signs it and keeps the copy."},
{"q":"«Explain the purpose of stock control for the depot's spare parts store.»","ans":[[5,"Stock Control"]],"exp":"Having parts when they are needed without tying up too much money: maximum, minimum and reorder levels, the supplier's lead time, getting rid of obsolete stock and preventing theft."},
{"q":"«Explain to the board what the transport manager is legally responsible for.»","ans":[[10,"Two types of transport manager"]],"exp":"Genuinely and continuously manage the transport: maintenance, hours and tachographs, drivers' licences, compliance with the licence and the law. If they fail, they can lose their good repute and be disqualified."},
{"q":"«A customer was undercharged on an invoice. Which document is sent to correct it?»","ans":[[6,"Credit and Debit Notes"]],"exp":"A debit note for the difference. If too much had been charged, it would be a credit note."},
{"q":"«After an accident in which a passenger was injured, what reports must be made, and to whom?»","ans":[[23,"Accident Reports"]],"exp":"Tell the Garda (there are injuries), notify the insurer as soon as possible and make the company's internal report with details, witnesses and photos."}]},"210":{"title":"Find it in the book 10","type":"buscar","questions":[{"q":"«A factory carries its own employees to and from work in its own minibus, driven by one of its staff. Does it need an operator licence?»","ans":[[10,"…types exempt from the licence (paragraph)"]],"exp":"No: carrying your own employees in company vehicles is exempt from the operator licence."},
{"q":"«How far may a driver reduce his daily rest, and how many times between two weekly rests?»","ans":[[11,"daily rest · double manned"]],"exp":"To 9 hours, a maximum of 3 times between two weekly rests. These reductions do not have to be compensated."},
{"q":"«Calculate the price of a 3-day hire: standing costs €320 a day, 900 km at €0.85 per km, plus €450 for the driver's overnight costs, with 15% profit added.»","ans":[[14,"Terminology"]],"exp":"3 × 320 = 960; 900 × 0.85 = 765; + 450 = €2,175 cost. +15% = 326.25 → price €2,501.25."},
{"q":"«The drivers' trade union is threatening to strike. What must happen before industrial action is lawful?»","ans":[[3,"Industrial Action"]],"exp":"A secret ballot of the members and at least one week's notice to the company. That way the union and the workers are protected by the law."},
{"q":"«What is a 'first use' inspection, and when should it be carried out?»","ans":[[13,"Daily Walkround Checks and First Use Inspections"]],"exp":"A check of the vehicle before it is used for the first time in the fleet (new, hired or after being off the road), as well as the driver's daily check."},
{"q":"«Can a driver who holds a Polish Category D licence drive the company's coaches?»","ans":[[21,"Foreign Drivers"]],"exp":"Yes: EU licences are valid in Ireland. They also need the Driver CPC, and the company must check that the licence is valid."},
{"q":"«35 driver duties are needed each day, 6 days a week, and each driver works 5 days. How many drivers are needed?»","ans":[[30,"Crew Rosters or Rotas"]],"exp":"35 × 6 = 210 shifts a week ÷ 5 = 42 drivers, plus holiday cover."},
{"q":"«Suggest KPIs to monitor the punctuality and reliability of the bus services.»","ans":[[8,"Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)"]],"exp":"% of departures on time, % of services operated out of those planned, breakdowns per 10,000 km and passenger complaints. Targets must be set and measured regularly."},
{"q":"«What are the main features of the digital and smart tachographs required under Regulation 165/2014?»","ans":[[12,"EU Regulation 165/2014"]],"exp":"They record on the vehicle unit and on the driver card. Smart ones record the satellite position and let inspectors read data remotely (DSRC) without stopping the vehicle."},
{"q":"«The company is being wound up. In what order are the creditors paid?»","ans":[[2,"Winding Up"],[2,"Liquidators"]],"exp":"First the liquidator's costs, then preferential debts (mainly wages), then non-preferential creditors and, if anything is left, the shareholders."},
{"q":"«Explain the difference between an improvement notice and a prohibition notice.»","ans":[[4,"Improvement / Prohibition notices, penalties"]],"exp":"Improvement notice: a breach must be corrected within a deadline. Prohibition notice: there is a serious and immediate risk and the activity must stop until it is fixed."},
{"q":"«Sales were €850,000 and direct costs €610,000. Calculate the gross profit and the gross profit as a percentage of sales.»","ans":[[5,"Trading Account"]],"exp":"850,000 − 610,000 = €240,000. Percentage: 240,000 × 100 ÷ 850,000 = 28.2%."},
{"q":"«What are the transport manager's responsibilities for motor tax across the fleet?»","ans":[[15,"Transport Manager Responsibilities"]],"exp":"Keep a register of the expiry dates of tax, insurance and roadworthiness tests, renew them on time and make sure no vehicle is driven without them."},
{"q":"«Where is parking prohibited for all vehicles?»","ans":[[22,"Parking Restrictions"]],"exp":"On clearways, motorways, bus lanes during their hours, pedestrian crossings and their zones, yellow lines and bus stops."},
{"q":"«Name the main types of international passenger service.»","ans":[[28,"Types of International Service"]],"exp":"Regular, special regular (for a specific group under contract), occasional and own-account (and shuttles)."},
{"q":"«What practical steps should a transport manager take to stay within competition law?»","ans":[[29,"Key Takeaways for Irish Transport Managers"]],"exp":"Set prices independently, do not discuss prices or bids with competitors, do not share customers or routes, be careful in trade associations and ask for advice if in doubt."},
{"q":"«After awarding a public bus contract, what details must the tendering authority publish?»","ans":[[31,"publish details of tenders"]],"exp":"The service contracted, who won and why, and the amounts of the highest and lowest bids."},
{"q":"«What do the 'E' route numbers on European roads mean?»","ans":[[32,"Route Numbers"]],"exp":"They are the main international routes (UN AGR agreement) and have the same number in every country, which makes planning the journey easier."},
{"q":"«How can technology monitor the mechanical condition of the coaches?»","ans":[[17,"The Vehicle"]],"exp":"Telematics connected to the CANbus: fault alerts, remote diagnostics, kilometres for maintenance, tyre pressure and consumption."},
{"q":"«What is the Commercial Vehicle Roadworthiness Risk rating, and how does it affect the operator?»","ans":[[20,"RSA inspection and enforcement regime"]],"exp":"The RSA scores each operator according to its results in roadworthiness tests, roadside checks and audits. With a poor rating (amber or red) it is stopped and inspected more often."},
{"q":"«What must be considered for loading passengers' luggage and carrying wheelchair users?»","ans":[[25,"Load Handling Considerations"]],"exp":"Luggage holds big enough and easy to load without injury, and for wheelchairs: a lift or ramp, a reserved space and anchorages."},
{"q":"«What are the penalties for breaking the drivers' hours rules, and who can be prosecuted?»","ans":[[11,"Offences and Penalties"]],"exp":"Both the driver and the company can be prosecuted: fines, court and a risk to good repute and the licence. The company is also responsible for what the driver does in other countries."},
{"q":"«Explain the terms 'cost centre' and 'cost unit'.»","ans":[[14,"Terminology"]],"exp":"Cost centre: what costs are charged to (the whole fleet, a group of vehicles or one vehicle). Cost unit: the measure used to compare, for example the cost per km or per passenger."},
{"q":"«What working time rules apply to the office and workshop staff who are not drivers?»","ans":[[3,"Working Time"]],"exp":"An average of 48 hours a week (over 4 months), a 15 min break after 4 h 30 and a 30 min break after 6 h, 11 hours of daily rest and 24 hours a week."},
{"q":"«Who may carry out safety inspections, and what are they expected to do?»","ans":[[13,"Safety Inspections"]],"exp":"Technically competent staff, in the company's own workshop or a contracted one. They check and record defects; they are not expected to repair while inspecting."},
{"q":"«What medical report is required when applying for a Category D licence?»","ans":[[21,"Medical Issues"]],"exp":"A medical report from a registered doctor (form D501) when applying for the licence. On renewal, from age 45, the report is also needed."},
{"q":"«A driver changes to a second coach during his working day. How is this recorded on the analogue chart?»","ans":[[12,"Centre field"]],"exp":"The details of the second vehicle (registration number, time of the change and odometer reading) are written on the chart already in use."},
{"q":"«What does a balance sheet show?»","ans":[[5,"Balance Sheet"]],"exp":"What the company has (assets), what it owes (liabilities) and the owners' capital on a specific date. It does not show the year's sales: that is the profit and loss account."},
{"q":"«Revenue has withdrawn the company's tax clearance. What is the effect on the operator licence?»","ans":[[10,"Transport Operator Licensing Requirements / Criteria (good repute, financial standing, competence, establishment)"]],"exp":"Being up to date with Revenue is part of good repute: the Minister can suspend or revoke the licence."},
{"q":"«What must a limited company do each year to remain in good standing with the Companies Registration Office?»","ans":[[2,"Companies — Commencement and Operation"]],"exp":"File the annual return (Annual Return, form B1) with the accounts, hold the annual general meeting and keep the registered office, directors and secretary up to date."}]}};
